.TH scalpel3 "1" "" "The scalpel3 File Carver" "The scalpel3 File Carver"

.SH NAME
scalpel3 \- Recover contiguous or fragmented data from an image file

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scalpel3
[\fB-a\fR]
[\fB-A\fR \fIsecs\fR]
[\fB-b\fR]
[\fB-B\fR]
[\fB-c\fR]
[\fB-C\fR]
[\fB-d\fR]
[\fB-D\fR \fIdisposition:filetype[,filetype]...\fR]
[\fB-e\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-F\fR \fIsecs\fR]
[\fB-g\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-G\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-h\fR]
[\fB-H\fR \fIphase\fR]
[\fB-i\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-I\fR \fIsecs\fR]
[\fB-j\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-k\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-L\fR]
[\fB-m\fR]
[\fB-M\fR]
[\fB-n\fR]
[\fB-o\fR \fIscalpel_output_dir\fR]
[\fB-O\fR]
[\fB-p\fR]
[\fB-P\fR]
[\fB-q\fR \fIclustersize\fR]
[\fB-R\fR]
[\fB-r\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-s\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-S\fR]
[\fB-t\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-u\fR \fI#\fR]
[\fB-U\fR \fIfiletype[,filetype]...\fR]
[\fB-v\fR]
[\fB-V\fR]
[\fB-w\fR]
[\fB-x\fR]
[\fB-y\fR \fItrue|false\fR]
[\fB-Y\fR \fIprovider[:devicelist]\fR]
[\fB-z\fR \fIfiletype[,filetype]...\fR]
img_filename
blockmap_filename

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Recover contiguous or fragmented data from an image file (typically sourced from a storage
device), using configurations for each file type. Create the associated blockmap with
\fBcrblockmap\fR(1) before carving. Options must precede the two filenames.

.SH OPTIONS
.PP
The following options are available:
Numeric arguments must be unsigned base-10 integers containing digits only;
signs, whitespace, suffixes, and values outside the documented range are
rejected.

.TP
.B -a
Reduce aggressive memory allocation. The default favors performance; use this
option to reduce memory use if allocation failures or excessive swapping occur.

.TP
.BI -A " #"
Create a checkpoint and stop execution after a specified number of seconds in the fragmented reassembly
phase of execution. This option is not checkpointed and must be specified on every execution where this behavior
is desired. Must be between 10 and 2147483647 seconds. The limit does not
include earlier phases; shutdown waits for a safe checkpoint.

.TP
.B -b
Write blockvectors.  Default is to not write blockvectors.

.TP
.B -B
Don't backtrack.  Default is backtracking on.  Useful only for debugging.

.TP
.B -c
Don't attempt fragmented recovery for any supported file type. This essentially
sets NO_DEFRAG to true for all file types. Block validators declared to support
only reassembly are skipped. MoDiCo remains independently controlled by
\fB-y\fR.

.TP
.B -C
Turn off IPC.  Generally not recommended, but useful in environments that
don't support Unix domain sockets. Default is on.

.TP
.B -d
Turn off header reuse for contiguous files.  Default is on and you should
generally leave this on.

.TP
.BI -D " disposition:filetype[,filetype]..."
Override when block validation runs for one or more file types.
\fBalways\fR runs the configured validator in all recovery modes,
\fBreassembly\fR skips it under \fB-c\fR, and \fBdisabled\fR never runs it.
For example, use \fB-D disabled:ELF\fR or
\fB-D reassembly:PNG,GIF\fR. This option may be specified more than once.
Repeating the same assignment is harmless, but conflicting assignments for
the same file type are rejected. The settings are checkpointed and cannot be
changed during checkpoint restore. When a validator does not run, confidence
values supplied by MoDiCo are preserved and any remaining unclassified
values default to valid.

.TP
.BI -e " #"
Override detected number of physical CPU cores. scalpel3 detects the number of
physical CPU cores for determining thread pool sizes. On systems with
hyperthreading, it may be beneficial to increase the number of perceived CPU
cores using -e.  On Apple Silicon systems, the number of performance cores is
used to size thread pools and -e can be used to experimentally increase the
sizes of thread pools. Must be between 1 and 65536. Explicit
\fB-r\fR, \fB-s\fR, \fB-t\fR, and \fB-u\fR settings override their
respective pool sizes; \fB-e\fR is not a process-wide thread limit.

.TP
.BI -F " #"
Create a checkpoint and stop execution when a new file hasn't been validated for
a specified number of seconds in the fragmented reassembly phase of
execution. This option is not checkpointed and must be specified on every
execution where this behavior is desired. Must be between 10 and 2147483647
seconds. Shutdown waits for a safe checkpoint.

.TP
.BI -g " #"
Set reassembly gallop factor. Accepts zero or a power of two; zero disables
galloping. The default is 16.

.TP
.BI -G " #"
Set reassembly gallop limit in blocks. Accepts zero or a power of two and
must be at least the gallop factor. The default is 16384.

.TP
.B -h
Print command-line help and then exit.

.TP
.BI -H " phase"
Stop after the required phase.  The supported phases are
\fBblock-validation\fR and \fBheader-footer\fR.
\fB-H block-validation\fR runs block classification as configured by
\fB-y\fR, \fB-D\fR, and the active file types,
writes \fIblockclassification.dat\fR, and exits before header/footer
searching.  \fB-H header-footer\fR performs the same block-validation phase,
then writes \fIheadersfooters.dat\fR and exits.  The latter mode therefore
produces both databases. These databases are also written during normal
carving; \fB-H\fR only selects where execution stops.
This option is not compatible with \fB-c\fR or \fB-R\fR.

.TP
.BI -i " #"
Set the number of newly validated files that triggers a periodic checkpoint
and blockmap swap during fragmented recovery.
This overrides VALIDATION_CP_THRESHOLD in scalpel.h.  This option
is not checkpointed and must be specified on every execution where this behavior is desired.
Must be between 1 and 65536; the default is 10000. Other checkpoint triggers
remain active.

.TP
.BI -I " secs"
Set the periodic checkpoint interval in seconds, overriding
PERIODIC_CHECKPOINTING_INTERVAL in scalpel.h (default 300). These checkpoints
synchronize work and blockmaps in memory; they are not recovery checkpoints.
An initial scheduling sweep uses 120-second intervals, and other triggers
can also cause synchronization. Recovery checkpoints are written separately
every 900 seconds during fragmented recovery.
This option is not checkpointed and must be specified again when resuming.
Must be between 5 and 65536. Intervals longer than 900 seconds cannot delay
the recovery checkpoint.

.TP
.BI -j " #"
Treat blocks in the image before the specified block number as covered.
This option in conjunction with -k allows execution of scalpel3 against a window of blocks in the input file.
Block numbers start at zero; the specified block is included in the window.

.TP
.BI -k " #"
Treat blocks in the image after the specified block number as covered.  This option in
conjunction with -j allows execution of scalpel3 against a window of blocks in the input file.
The specified block is included in the window. Both endpoints must be in
the image, with the start no greater than the end.

.TP
.B -L
Provide a brief list of supported file types and exit.

.TP
.B -m
Perform memory profiling.  Produces lots of output.

.TP
.B -M
Disable shadow blockmap peeking for block selection.  The default is on. Only
useful for debugging.

.TP
.B -n
Disable backtrace on crash. The default is to generate backtraces and this
should generally be left on.

.TP
.BI -o " scalpel_output_dir"
Set output directory for carved files. Only one active scalpel3 process may
use an output directory at a time. Scalpel3 enforces this with a persistent
\fI.scalpel3.lock\fR file in the base output directory. The file may remain
after Scalpel3 exits; only its operating system lock, not its presence,
indicates that an invocation is active. Newly created Scalpel3 directories
and files are accessible only to the invoking user (modes 0700 and 0600,
respectively). Permissions may be changed externally when output must be
shared.

.TP
.B -O
Omit the numbered subdirectories for each file type. The VALIDATED,
PROMISING, and INPROGRESS directories remain. The default is to organize
output by file type.

.TP
.B -p
Turn on file type prioritization for fragmented file reassembly.  Default is off.

.TP
.B -P
Write INPROGRESS files on every checkpoint.  The default is only to write
INPROGRESS when requested via IPC (e.g., scalpel3\-ctl \-c).  This is
primarily for debugging, as it adds substantial overhead.

.TP
.BI -q " clustersize"
Specify block/cluster size. Default is 512 bytes. Must be between 512 and
1073741824 bytes, inclusive, and a multiple of 512.

.TP
.B -R
Resume from checkpoint in the output directory selected by \fB-o\fR.
Saved recovery settings are restored; do not repeat options such as
\fB-q\fR, \fB-w\fR, \fB-y\fR, \fB-D\fR, \fB-U\fR, or \fB-z\fR.
Thread counts, the execution provider, and checkpoint/stop intervals can
be selected for the resumed run. Supply the image and blockmap filenames
again and retain the executable that created the checkpoint.

.TP
.BI -r " #"
Specify the maximum number of threads per filemirror pool (1 through 65536).
The default is the detected core count, or the value supplied with \fB-e\fR.

.TP
.BI -s " #"
Specify the maximum number of reassembly threads (1 through 65536).
The default is the detected core count, or the value supplied with \fB-e\fR.

.TP
.B -S
Turn off reassembly thread work sharing. Default is on.

.TP
.BI -t " #"
Specify the maximum number of search threads (1 through 65536).
The default is the detected core count, or the value supplied with \fB-e\fR.

.TP
.BI -u " #"
Specify the maximum number of validation threads (1 through 65536).
The default is the detected core count, or the value supplied with \fB-e\fR.

.TP
.BI -U " filetype[,filetype]..."
Deactivate carving for one or more file types, e.g., \fB-U JPG\fR or
\fB-U JPG,PNG\fR. This option may be specified more than once.
Names are case insensitive and must match the list from \fB-L\fR;
duplicate names are rejected, including across repeated options.
Not compatible with \fB-z\fR.

.TP
.B -v
Verbose mode.

.TP
.B -V
Print copyright information and exit.

.TP
.B -w
Write PROMISING candidates as well as VALIDATED files. PROMISING output may
contain useful partial files or complete files for which the validator lacks
enough evidence to declare success. It may also contain incorrect
reconstructions and should be independently examined. The default omits
PROMISING payloads to save disk space.

.TP
.B -x
Override checkpoint integrity checks. Use this only if you know what you're doing!


.TP
.BI -y " true|false"
Explicitly enable or disable MoDiCo block prioritization. MoDiCo is enabled by
default; use \fB-y false\fR to disable it. This option is checkpointed and
cannot be changed during checkpoint restore.

.TP
.BI -Y " provider[:devicelist]"
Select the ONNX execution provider (and GPUs) used by all AI components for
this run: \fBcpu\fR, \fBcuda\fR, \fBcoreml\fR, or \fBauto\fR, with an optional
CUDA device list, e.g. \fB-Y cuda:0,2\fR. The default (\fBauto\fR) uses all
healthy NVIDIA GPUs, CoreML on Apple Silicon, and CPU when no accelerator is
present. A requested or detected accelerator that cannot be initialized is a
fatal error rather than a silent CPU run; use \fB-Y cpu\fR to intentionally
select CPU inference. When the \fBCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\fR environment variable is set, it
filters and reorders the CUDA devices as usual, and \fB-Y\fR device ids name
the visible devices. When \fB-Y\fR selects a proper subset of the GPUs,
scalpel3 restricts CUDA visibility to those devices so inference and runtime
allocations stay on the selected GPUs.
.sp
On Apple Silicon, \fBcoreml\fR uses the matching graph-cut MoDiCo model by
default when it is installed, otherwise it uses the original model.
\fBcoreml-legacy\fR selects the original MoDiCo model for comparison
or rollback, and \fBcoreml-graphcut\fR explicitly requires the graph-cut model.
These two selectors still use the normal CoreML provider for other AI
components; they only change which MoDiCo artifact is loaded.


.TP
.BI -z " filetype[,filetype]..."
Carve only one or more specified file types, e.g., \fB-z JPG\fR or
\fB-z JPG,PNG\fR. This option may be specified more than once. Not compatible
with \fB-U\fR. Names are case insensitive and must match \fB-L\fR;
duplicate names are rejected, including across repeated options.

.SH OUTPUT DATABASES
After block validation completes, scalpel3 atomically writes
\fIblockclassification.dat\fR in the output directory.  It contains a
versioned header, the block size and block count, the final file type and
subtype mapping, and the column-major block confidence table produced by
MoDiCo and the regular block validators.  Duplicate blocks are represented by
their exemplars in the associated blockmap.
.PP
The classification database is a persistent output artifact even when
checkpointing is disabled.  Restartable checkpoints depend on this file and
include it in checkpoint integrity verification; it must be retained with the
checkpoint files for a later \fB-R\fR restore.
.PP
Restartable state is stored in two alternating checkpoint slots.  A new slot
is written and verified completely before it is atomically published, and the
previously published slot is retained as a fallback.  If publication is
interrupted or the published slot fails verification, \fB-R\fR selects the
newest valid slot.  The image blockmap is deliberately external to the
checkpoint transaction so it may be updated independently; restored
candidates are reconciled with the blockmap supplied to the resumed run.
.PP
After header/footer searching completes, scalpel3 writes
\fIheadersfooters.dat\fR in the output directory. Use \fBdumpbt\fR(1) and
\fBdumphf\fR(1) to inspect the respective databases.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
Unlike previous versions of scalpel, scalpel3 requires recompilation
(and commonly, custom code) to support new file types.  The file
"scalpelconf.c" contains configurations for the file types that are supported
by scalpel3.  Please see this source file for more information.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_HOME
Set this to the base scalpel3 directory so runtime resources, including AI
models, can be located.  It does not select the executable: checkpoint
compatibility uses the hash of the scalpel3 binary that is actually running.
This variable is not required for the informational \fB-h\fR, \fB-L\fR, and
\fB-V\fR options.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_NO_MODICO
If set, disable MoDiCo unless an explicit \fB-y\fR option overrides it.
Other block validators are unaffected.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_DIR
Override the model directory, normally
\fI${SCALPEL3_HOME}/src/exe_vision/unix\fR.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_CLASSMAP
Override the class-name mapping file, normally \fIclass_names.json\fR in
the MoDiCo model directory.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_INTRA_THREADS
Set ONNX Runtime's intra-op thread count for MoDiCo. Zero lets ONNX Runtime
choose. By default, CPU inference uses at most 12 logical cores; accelerator
providers use zero. This is separate from Scalpel3's thread pools.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_CACHE
Override the pathname of the reusable MoDiCo confidence cache. By default,
scalpel3 uses the input blockmap pathname with \fI.modico\fR appended. Set this
variable to \fBoff\fR, \fBfalse\fR, \fBno\fR, or \fB0\fR to disable cache reuse.
The cache is accepted only when its evidence image, initial blockmap, model,
execution provider, confidence policy, and file type mapping all match the
current run. Regular block validators still run after a cache hit.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_TENSORRT
On Linux with CUDA, scalpel3 automatically uses FP32 TensorRT for standard
native-histogram MoDiCo models with block sizes of 4096 bytes or larger when a
compatible cached engine exists or the expected workload is large enough to
repay a cold engine build. Smaller block sizes and short cold workloads use
native CUDA. Set this variable to \fBoff\fR, \fBfalse\fR, \fBno\fR, \fBnone\fR,
or \fB0\fR to force native CUDA; any other value forces TensorRT for comparison.
If TensorRT is unavailable, scalpel3 falls back to CUDA without disabling
MoDiCo.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_TENSORRT_CACHE
Override the root directory for persistent TensorRT engine and timing caches.
Cache entries are separated by model hash, ONNX Runtime version, TensorRT
version, GPU identity, and inference profile. A disabled value bypasses
TensorRT and uses CUDA directly.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_TENSORRT_CACHE_MAX_MIB
Set the maximum total size of the persistent TensorRT cache. The default is
512 MiB. Inactive least recently used entries are removed when necessary.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_TENSORRT_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS
Set the maximum age of an inactive TensorRT cache entry. The default is 30
days.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_TENSORRT_COLD_MIN_BLOCKS
Override the expected-block threshold for building a cold TensorRT engine.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_COREML_CACHE_DIR
Override the compiled CoreML model cache directory, normally
\fI$HOME/Library/Caches/scalpel3/modico-coreml\fR. An empty value,
\fBoff\fR, \fBfalse\fR, \fBnone\fR, or \fB0\fR disables this cache,
not CoreML inference.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_COREML_CACHE_MAX_MIB
Set the maximum total size of the persistent Core ML cache. The default is
512 MiB.
.TP
.B SCALPEL3_MODICO_COREML_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS
Set the maximum age of an inactive Core ML cache entry. The default is 30
days. Active TensorRT and Core ML sessions are protected from cache pruning.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero after successful carving and for the informational \fB-h\fR,
\fB-L\fR, and \fB-V\fR options.  Returns nonzero when an error prevents or
terminates execution.

